<p>Shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remains heavily constrained despite the US-Iran ceasefire. Multiple reports say Iran still controls passage, and that even some transits on the revised route require coordination with Iranian forces. President Trump said the restrictions did not match the ceasefire understanding and warned Iran not to impose any toll on ships using the waterway. Because so much energy trade depends on the passage, the slow reopening matters far beyond the Gulf. Dawn reported that traffic fell to well below 10% of normal levels on Thursday, with only seven ships passing in 24 hours compared with roughly 140 in ordinary conditions, while peace talks due in Islamabad on April 10 offered a possible path to easing the standoff.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/t1YBqqGHdPSoRiQaLH72QKtBivISWXQdMynHik_zntqEcbKRehHff04dZiEMyAIt62L4d7UjHD9UbJYDzKs2KbRJ3HvptW28qk2aU--I8vTU' alt='A cargo ship in the Gulf, near the Strait of Hormuz, as seen from northern Ras al-Khaimah.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Selective reopening: The few ships that crossed were mostly dry bulk carriers, not a broad return of tanker traffic, showing that movement has resumed only unevenly.</li><li>Territorial-waters directive: Iran told ships to stay in its own waters, underscoring that any reopening is happening on Tehran’s terms.</li><li>Price strain: Some physical oil grades hit fresh record highs on Thursday as the shipping bottleneck persisted after the ceasefire announcement.</li></ul><blockquote>Iran is doing a very poor job, dishonorable some would say, of allowing Oil to go through the Strait of Hormuz - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian authorities and IRGC: Tehran has publicly maintained that the strait is open, while in practice some passage still depends on coordination with Iranian military forces. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/why-us-iran-ceasefire-has-not-led-to-hormuz-reopening'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Shipping-dependent countries: Governments that need access are being pushed into ship-by-ship arrangements with Iran, a process that can clash with Washington’s stance even after the ceasefire. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/10/world/middleeast/strait-hormuz-iran-ships-oil.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>ADNOC: The UAE state oil company’s chief publicly urged Iran to reopen the strait without conditions, reflecting the commercial urgency for Gulf exporters. (<a href='https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/09/iran-war-oil-strait-hormuz-tanker-ship-uae.html'>CNBC</a>)</li><li>Maritime security experts: Security specialists warned that ships without Iranian authorization still face danger even after the truce, helping explain why vessel owners remain cautious about resuming normal schedules. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990371/hormuz-traffic-plummets-as-iran-asserts-control'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/10/world/middleeast/strait-hormuz-iran-ships-oil.html'>No Longer Off Limits, the Strait of Hormuz Remains Thorny Politically</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/why-us-iran-ceasefire-has-not-led-to-hormuz-reopening'>Why US-Iran ceasefire has not led to Hormuz reopening</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990371/hormuz-traffic-plummets-as-iran-asserts-control'>Hormuz traffic plummets as Iran asserts control</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/09/iran-war-oil-strait-hormuz-tanker-ship-uae.html'>Abu Dhabi National Oil Company CEO Sultan Al Jaber says that the strait is still not open because Iran has been restricting and controlling traffic.</a> - cnbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-israel-iran-war-live-updates-us-iran-ceasefire-missile-strikes-middle-east-crisis-conflict-live-news-iran-drone-attack-trump-ceasefire-strait-of-ho-11325915#publisher=newsstand'